<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nara Narayan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1540">1540</a>–<a href="/years/1587">1587</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> Narayan was a king of the Koch dynasty whose reign, from 1540 to 1587, was the high point of the Koch kingdom in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. With his brother and general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilarai">Chilarai</a> he campaigned against the Ahom, Kachari, Jaintia and other neighbours. He patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning and the Vaishnava teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srimanta-sankardev">Sankardev</a> and rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakhya-temple">Kamakhya</a> temple. Late in his reign the kingdom was divided, creating Koch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajo">Hajo</a> in the east and leaving Koch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nara-narayan.jpg" alt="Nara Narayan" width="219" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist and scribe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_Raja_Nara_Narayan,_from_a_manuscript_illustration_of_Raja_Nara_Narayan_of_the_Koch_Dynasty_of_the_Kamata_Kingdom_seated_on_his_throne_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nara Narayan, first known as Malladev, was a son of the Koch king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biswa-singha">Biswa Singha</a>. When his father died, Malladev and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilarai">Chilarai</a> were in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, and another brother, Nara Singha, took the throne. The two hurried back and, with the help of supporters among the courtiers, seized it; Nara Singha fled to the Morung kingdom, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Malladev took the title Narayana, which became the dynastic title of the kings after him, issued coins and had his seal made. He appointed Chilarai, then known as Sukladhwaj, yuvaraj and commander-in-chief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Koch kingdom was then a tributary of the Ahom kingdom. A border quarrel at Sala grew into war in 1546, and in 1547 the Ahom king Suklenmung defeated the Koch army at the Battle of Pichala. Nara Narayan prepared the next campaign slowly: he sent a mission to the Ahom court in 1555, had his step-brother Kamal Narayan build the Gohain Kamal Ali road along the foothills to the eastern frontier, and won the support of tribal groups and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a>. In late 1562 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilarai">Chilarai</a> led a force of 60,000 east, and by April 1563 the Ahom king Sukhaamphaa had abandoned his capital. The Treaty of Majuli established Koch supremacy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley.</p><p>The Kachari king Durlabhnarayan submitted without a fight, followed by the Manipuri king, while the Jaintia king fought and was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilarai">Chilarai</a> also campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripura">Tripura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a>. The expansion was halted when the Koch faced Suleiman Karrani, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Nara Narayan introduced a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coin, the Narayani, that shaped the coinage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>.</p><p>In his reign a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> named Siddhantavagisa introduced the fourfold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> system at the court. Because the new religious order clashed with the beliefs of the Koch, Mech and Kachari peoples, the king ordered that the religious practices of the different groups be recognised. He employed scholars to translate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> into Assamese and to compile works on arithmetic, astronomy and grammar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1581 Raghu Deva, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilarai">Chilarai</a>, became de facto ruler of the eastern part, Koch Hajo, under his uncle's suzerainty, and he declared independence after Nara Narayan's death in 1587. Nara Narayan's son Lakshmi Narayan inherited only the western part, Koch Bihar. Nara Narayan was the last ruler of the undivided Koch kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nara Narayan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Koch kingdom rose under Biswa Singha in the early 16th century and reached its height under <b>Nara Narayan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cooch-behar-state">Cooch Behar State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Koch kingdom reached its height under <b>Nara Narayan</b> and his brother and general Chilarai in the mid 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/koch-hajo">Koch Hajo</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Nara Narayan</b> (r. 1540–1587) and his brother and general Chilarai, the Koch defeated the Ahom kingdom and, according to later chronicles, forced tribute from the Kachari kingdom, Jaintia, Manipur and Tripura.</blockquote><a href="/state/koch-kingdom">Koch Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
